# Forward-Looking Fixes

- Default to forward-looking fixes rather than restoring stale or legacy behavior.
- Do not reintroduce removed, outdated, or superseded behavior only because old tests, docs, or habits expected it.
- Restore backward compatibility only when the user explicitly asks for it or when it is clearly required by a current documented contract or active consumer.
- When behavior has drifted, prefer preserving the newer, cleaner contract if it aligns with project goals.
- Prefer updating stale tests and docs to match current intended behavior instead of regressing implementation to satisfy old assumptions.
- If a change is intentionally breaking, call it out clearly and keep the migration path minimal and explicit.
